WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:03.100
-Hi!
-Stunner. Stunner, as always.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.300
Welcome back.
-Thank you. Thank you, guys.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.934
-It is so great to see you.

00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:09.033
-It's so good to be seen
and to see you.

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:11.934
-And to see you, as well.
And always looking gorgeous.

00:00:11.934 --> 00:00:13.367
But look at you looking gorgeous
on the cover of

00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:14.501
"Marie Claire" magazine
right there.

00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:16.167
-Oh, my goodness!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:18.667
-Ooh-la-la! Cover girl!

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:19.801
-It's a lot of work
went into that.

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:20.934
A lot of magician --
hair and makeup.

00:00:20.934 --> 00:00:22.601
-No, I love this. Yeah.
-Yes.

00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:23.601
-Well, you had --
Congratulations.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:24.901
You had a big weekend.
-I did.

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:27.701
-You just got honored at the
American Black Film --

00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:29.601
-Festival Honors. Yes.

00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:31.467
-This is for --
-Thank you. That's very kind.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.534
[ Cheers and applause ]
-This is big.

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:35.467
-Yeah.
-Excellence in the Arts Award.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.100
-Yes.
-Can you explain?

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.934
Everyone was talking about
your red-carpet look.

00:00:38.934 --> 00:00:40.667
And it's very --
[ Crowd cheering ]

00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:42.234
I know -- stunning.
-Thank you.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:44.267
So, this dress
was really special

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:45.701
because this is
an archival dress.

00:00:45.701 --> 00:00:49.000
It was a dress worn by
Whitney Houston in 1996.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.634
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Now, I mean, how --

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:52.701
-Just crazy.

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:53.701
-But how do you even
think of that up?

00:00:53.701 --> 00:00:54.734
Who thinks of that --

00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:56.601
-So, her -- The designer,
Marc Bouwer,

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:58.300
had it in his archives.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.567
And it had not been worn.

00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:02.400
And in conversations,
my stylist,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.501
my amazing stylist, Law --
he was talking to the designer.

00:01:05.501 --> 00:01:07.400
And I was like, "Oh,
it's such an incredible dress.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.734
It has to be the right moment."

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:11.133
And, so, if you're going to talk
about Black Excellence,

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.334
that's Whitney Houston.

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:13.801
So that was
the moment to honor her.

00:01:13.801 --> 00:01:15.267
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:16.801
-To honor her. Yes.

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:18.734
-What did it feel like
wearing it?

00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:20.667
-I felt like a diva!
-Yeah.

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:22.100
-Yes!

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.968
-I have a picture of Whitney
performing in this dress here.

00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:26.567
Look at that.
-I know, right?

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:28.033
Isn't that incredible?
-I mean, is that unbelievable?

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:30.300
You posted a very fun video

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.467
about wearing the dress
on your Instagram.

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:32.801
-I did.

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:33.901
-I just want to show everybody,
because I loved it.

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:35.667
-Oh, gosh.
-Let's take a look at this.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.968
-What's cooler than wearing
Whitney Houston's dress?

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:40.000
-♪ Nothing ♪

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.267
♪ Nothing ♪

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.234
♪ Nothing ♪

00:01:43.234 --> 00:01:44.567
-Come on!

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:46.801
I love you! Come on!
That's the best.

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:48.000
"Nothing."

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.601
-You got to get the shake,
the Whitney shake.

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:51.000
-The shake, yeah.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.434
Today is
International Women's Day.

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:53.601
-Happy
International Women's Day.

00:01:53.601 --> 00:01:55.200
-And I'm so happy
you're on the show tonight.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.868
-Thank you. Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:58.634
Do you know in like
more than 20 countries,

00:01:58.634 --> 00:02:01.267
it's an actual national holiday,
a public holiday,

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:02.267
where people get the day off?

00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:03.701
-Ooh! Let's --

00:02:03.701 --> 00:02:05.701
-I've been whispering that to
all the women on your staff.

00:02:05.701 --> 00:02:08.000
-Yeah. Everyone
is walking out now.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.400
-Everyone in that picture.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.467
Exactly.
Everybody has the day off.

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:12.601
"No, please come back. Please.

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:13.667
I need you, I need you,
I need you."

00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:15.200
-They'll be back tomorrow.
-Yeah.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.300
You were telling me backstage

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.501
that you're working on
your memoir.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:18.767
-Yes.

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:20.000
-And I want to talk to you
about that, because --

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.434
-Okay.

00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:23.267
-What's it like?
And how far are you?

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:25.701
And how did you get the
inspiration to --

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:29.300
-I guess when "Scandal"
was ending, it was really

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.968
kind of a turning point
in my life.

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:32.434
It was sort of the end
of an era.

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:35.334
And I spent some time
learning more about myself,

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:37.667
and it just made me
want to share

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.100
some of what I was learning.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.200
But I don't -- I mean,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.200
I'm at the point
where I'm in final edits.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.667
But it's true,
that thing they say,

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.200
that you're never
really done writing.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.901
They just take it away
from you at some point.

00:02:46.901 --> 00:02:49.033
Like, I'm at the deadline,
so I have to hand it over.

00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:51.767
But I'm just, like,
so obsessed with every word

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:55.300
and wanting to make sure that
every single feeling and word

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.734
and sentiment and comma and
period is in the right place.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:02:59.300
-It's called
"Thicker Than Water"

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.434
is the title.

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:02.400
-Yes.
-What's the background?

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.501
-It's a lot about family, and --
-It is?

00:03:04.501 --> 00:03:06.000
-Yeah.
And what family means to me.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.200
And -- Yeah.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.734
-And do you have your family,
then your career,

00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:09.968
because, I mean,
I know a little bit about you,

00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:11.267
but I want to know more.

00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:12.801
-Well, you'll get it.
You'll have to buy the book.

00:03:12.801 --> 00:03:14.200
-Yeah. Alright, I will, I will.
-No spoilers.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.267
-I will totally do it.
-No spoilers!

00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:17.000
-Yeah, no spoilers here, no.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.434
-But family is
really important to me.

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:20.067
I mean, even with this show,
"Unprisoned," like,

00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:24.801
I'm really -- I think
I'm very interested in how

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:29.000
we find family and create family
and what family means to us.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.501
And so that's a lot of
what my work is

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:32.701
centering around these days.

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:34.868
-I like "Unprisoned"
because it's a drama --

00:03:34.868 --> 00:03:36.868
it's a family drama,
but it's funny.

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.601
-It's a dramedy for sure.

00:03:38.601 --> 00:03:39.634
-Yes, it's a dramedy.
-Yes, yes, yes.

00:03:39.634 --> 00:03:41.133
-That's a good word for it.

00:03:41.133 --> 00:03:43.033
-And, I mean, I mostly do a lot
of drama, but you know, like,

00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:45.167
the best week of my life,
outside of my honeymoon,

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.434
was my week on "SNL."

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.067
-You were fantastic.
-Like, I love doing comedy.

00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:51.334
-You're great at being funny.
-Thank you, coming from you.

00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:54.033
So, I really love this show,
because it's about kind of

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:56.667
meaningful, heartfelt moments,

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.601
but it's very, very funny,
as well.

00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:00.200
-Can you set up
what the show is about?

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.300
-Sure. I better be able to.
-Yeah.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.901
-So, it's about a dad.

00:04:03.901 --> 00:04:05.200
The dad is really
the center of the show.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.200
It's about a dad and a daughter
and a grandson.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.767
Now, obviously, I'm neither
a father nor a grandson,

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:09.767
so I'm the daughter.

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:11.100
-Yes, of course.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.834
-And my dad has been in and out
of prison my whole life.

00:04:13.834 --> 00:04:16.000
And the show really is about
what happens

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.667
when he is released from prison.

00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:19.434
So it's about the experience of
what it's like to be

00:04:19.434 --> 00:04:22.033
a formerly incarcerated person
and also what it's like

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:24.000
to love
a formerly incarcerated person

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.534
who's re-entering society.

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:29.234
And, you know,
there are 80 million Americans

00:04:29.234 --> 00:04:30.634
living with criminal records.

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:33.434
So this show is
really about so many of us,

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:35.434
because either we have
a criminal record

00:04:35.434 --> 00:04:37.701
or many of us love somebody who
has a criminal record,

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:40.868
and that impacts our lives
in very deep ways.

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:43.801
But, also, really,
there are very funny moments.

00:04:43.801 --> 00:04:45.767
-Yeah. And how do you
come back into society?

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:47.300
And how do you deal with family
and all that stuff?

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.400
And Delroy Lindo.
-Delroy Lindo is brilliant.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.200
-Yeah, we love him.
-He plays the dad on the show.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.834
He's so spectacular. I love him.
-Was he your first choice?

00:04:55.834 --> 00:04:57.167
-He was my only choice.
-Oh, really?

00:04:57.167 --> 00:04:58.367
-He was my only choice.

00:04:58.367 --> 00:04:59.968
When I read the script
and came on as a producer

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:01.400
and started developing it,
I was like,

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.000
"There is one guy
that I see in this role."

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.100
He didn't know,
but he was the one.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.167
-Yeah.

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:07.634
-And, so,
then I did a Zoom with him.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.300
This was back
when we were only Zooming.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.167
And I talked to him and kind of
convinced him that

00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:14.501
what we were doing
was going to be important.

00:05:14.501 --> 00:05:17.000
I had seen him in "Da 5 Bloods"
and had --

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.133
have been obsessed with him
for a long time.

00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:19.567
So I called Spike Lee
to get his number.

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:20.934
And then I called him,
and then he said, "Yes."

00:05:20.934 --> 00:05:22.133
And I was like,
"We have a show!"

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:24.434
-Yes! We have a show!

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:25.534
And I want to show a clip.

00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:27.834
Here is Kerry Washington
in "Unprisoned."

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:29.300
-Yes.
-Take a look.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.300
-Ready?
-Yeah.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.000
Ow! Oh, hallelujah!

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.434
Whoo!

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:37.567
-Once a week, check-ins for drug
testing and career counseling.

00:05:37.567 --> 00:05:40.534
And I assume
he'll be living with you.

00:05:40.534 --> 00:05:41.934
-Oh, no. That's a no go.
-Oh, no, absolutely not.

00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:44.634
-Well, we do recommend
parolees live with family

00:05:44.634 --> 00:05:47.467
to give them the best chance
of staying out.

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:49.267
-Yeah. No, of course.
That makes sense.

00:05:49.267 --> 00:05:51.100
But I-I only have two rules,

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.334
and one of them is that
he cannot live me.

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:53.400
The other is,
don't ask me for money.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.801
-And don't ask for money.

00:05:55.801 --> 00:05:57.501
But what my daughter
doesn't know is that

00:05:57.501 --> 00:05:59.434
I'm out for good this time.

00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:03.334
And you -- you're going to see,
for your last --

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:07.434
-I have heard this before --
in 1986, 1992, 1998 --

00:06:07.434 --> 00:06:12.701
actually, December of '97,
and then most famously in 2005.

00:06:12.701 --> 00:06:15.234
-Yeah. Come on.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:16.734
Kerry Washington, everybody.

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:18.934
Stream all episodes
of "Unprisoned"

00:06:18.934 --> 00:06:20.300
this Friday on Hulu.

